public static Class<?>[] resolveArguments(Type genericType, Class<?> targetType) {
    if (!(genericType instanceof ParameterizedType)) {
        return null;
    }

   .ParameterizedType parameterizedType = (ParameterizedType) genericType;
    Type[] actualTypeArguments = parameterizedType.getActualTypeArguments();

    if (actualTypeArguments.length != targetType.getTypeParameters().length) {
        return null;
    }

    List<Class<?>> resolvedArguments = new ArrayList<>();
    for (int i = 0; i < actualTypeArguments.length; i++) {
        Class<?> actualArgument = actualTypeArguments[i];
        if (actualArgument instanceof GenericType<G>) {
            actualArgument = ((GenericType<G>) actualArgument).resolve(targetType);
        } else if (actualArgument instanceof KeyValue<?, ?>) {
            actualArgument = actualArgument.getType();
        }
        resolvedArguments.add(actualArgument);
    }

    Class<?>[] resolvedArray = resolvedArguments.toArray(new Class<?>[0]);
    return resolvedArray;
}
```

In this solution, the method `resolveArguments` takes a `Type` object `genericType` and a `Class<?>` object `targetType` as parameters. It first checks if `genericType` is a parameterized type. If not, it returns `null`. Then, it retrieves the actual type arguments from the `ParameterizedType` object.

Next, it checks if the number of actual type arguments matches the number of type parameters in `targetType`. If not, it also returns `null`.

If both conditions are met, it iterates over the actual type arguments and resolves them. If the actual type argument is of type `GenericType<G>`, it resolves it by calling the `resolve` method provided. If the actual type argument is of type `KeyValue<?, ?>`, it retrieves the type itself.

Finally, it converts the list of resolved arguments to an array of `Class<?>` and returns it.

